<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabonidus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 556–<a href="/years/539-bce">539 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nabonidus was the last king of the Chaldean or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>. He alienated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>'s religious establishment by neglecting the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> and spent about a decade at the Arabian oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayma">Tayma</a>, during which the New Year festival was not held. His unpopularity contributed to the empire's fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 539 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabonidus.jpg" alt="Nabonidus" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail,_stele_of_Nabonidus._Nabonidus_wears_the_traditional_dress_of_a_Babylonian_king,_holding_objects_symbolizing_justice_and_power.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Four kings — Amel-Marduk, Neriglissar, Labashi-Marduk and <b>Nabonidus</b> — took the throne in the six years after Nebuchadnezzar's death in 562 BCE, most of the successions involving assassination or coup.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Croesus built a network of alliances with Amasis of Egypt, <b>Nabonidus</b> of Babylon and Sparta, which he hoped would protect him against the rising power of Persia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The treasures are now divided between the British Museum, the Penn Museum and the Baghdad Museum, and the partly restored ziggurat, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-Dagan of Isin, Kurigalzu I and <b>Nabonidus</b>, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Edom survived the fall of Assyria but lost its independence to <b>Nabonidus</b> of the Chaldean Empire, who campaigned there c. 552 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/edom">Edom</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-6th century BCE the Babylonian king <b>Nabonidus</b> campaigned in the Hejaz and listed Dedan among the oases he took while residing at Tayma.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dedan">Kingdom of Dedan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Carmania (Satrapy), in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.87,39.03&amp;year=-548" aria-label="Harran on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5529 835 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nabonidus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5979 1105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5979 1105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.87,39.03&amp;year=-548">What was here in 548 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
